---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Histogram from "../../components/Histogram.astro";

const title = "Moving Averages Explained — SMA, EMA and the 200 DMA";
const description =
  "How simple and exponential moving averages differ, what the 20/50/100/200-day " +
  "averages are used for, crossovers, and why they lag by construction.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Technical analysis / Moving averages">
  <h1>Moving averages</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A moving average is the average closing price over the last <em>n</em> days,
    recalculated each day. It is the simplest way to strip daily noise out of a
    price series and see the underlying direction — and the oldest, which is part
    of why it still matters.
  </p>

  <h2>Simple versus exponential</h2>
  <div class="formula">SMA = (P₁ + P₂ + ... + Pₙ) / n          every day weighted equally

EMA today = (Price × k) + (EMA yesterday × (1 − k))
where k = 2 / (n + 1)                   recent days weighted more</div>
  <p>
    An SMA treats a price from 200 days ago exactly like yesterday's. An EMA
    weights recent data more heavily, so it turns sooner. Neither is correct —
    they answer slightly different questions, and EMA's faster response comes with
    more false turns.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    This site uses <strong>EMAs</strong> (21, 50 and 200) on the
    <a href="/screener/">screener</a>. The "200 DMA" that gets quoted in market
    commentary is usually a simple 200-day average, so the two will not agree
    exactly. Neither is wrong; they are different calculations and worth not
    confusing.
  </p>

  <h2>What each period is used for</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>20_21_day</code><span class="meta">about a trading month</span></div>
      <p>Short-term direction. Price oscillating around it means no short-term
        trend. Often used as a reference for pullbacks within an existing uptrend.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>50_day</code><span class="meta">about a quarter</span></div>
      <p>Medium-term trend. The most watched intermediate average, which is part
        of why it appears to "work" — a great many participants are looking at the
        same line.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>200_day</code><span class="meta">about a year of trading</span></div>
      <p>The conventional dividing line between a long-term uptrend and
        downtrend. Its influence is substantially self-fulfilling: it matters
        because everyone watches it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Crossovers</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>golden_cross</code><span class="meta">50 crosses above 200</span></div>
      <p>Read as a shift to a longer-term uptrend. It is also a <em>lagging</em>
        event by definition — the 50-day only crosses the 200-day well after price
        has already moved.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>death_cross</code><span class="meta">50 crosses below 200</span></div>
      <p>The mirror image. Both get heavy media coverage, and both describe
        something that has already happened rather than predicting what comes next.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>faster_crossovers</code><span class="meta">21 over 50</span></div>
      <p>Trigger much sooner and far more often — including many times in a
        sideways market where price keeps crossing back and forth. The screener's
        <span class="c">ema21_above_50</span> condition is this comparison.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <Histogram
    field="pct_from_52wh" min={-60} max={0} bins={24}
    markers={[-25]}
    bands={[{ from: -25, to: 0, label: "within 25% of high", tone: "good" }]}
    title="Distance from the 52-week high across the universe" unit="%"
    caption="A breadth reading in one picture. When the distribution hugs zero the market is broadly strong; when it spreads left, few stocks are near their highs regardless of what the index is doing." />

  <h2>The lag is unavoidable</h2>
  <p>
    Every moving average is built from past prices, so it must trail the current
    price. A 200-day average includes data from ten months ago. There is a direct
    trade-off and no way around it:
  </p>
  <div class="formula">shorter average → responds faster → more false signals
longer average  → responds slower → fewer signals, later</div>
  <p class="lede">
    Anyone claiming to have found the period that gets both has usually found the
    period that fit the past best, which is a different thing. See
    <a href="/learn/overfitting/">overfitting</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where they fail</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>sideways_markets</code><span class="meta">the main weakness</span></div>
      <p>In a range, price crosses the average constantly and every crossover is a
        false signal. Checking <a href="/learn/adx/">ADX</a> first tells you
        whether a trend exists at all.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>gaps</code><span class="meta">structural</span></div>
      <p>A stock that gaps down 15% on news is far below its average before any
        average can respond. Moving averages offer no protection against events.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>corporate_actions</code><span class="meta">data issue</span></div>
      <p>Splits and bonuses break an unadjusted price series and make the average
        meaningless until it works through. Data on this site is adjusted, but it
        is worth knowing the failure mode exists.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <h2>On the screener</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Two of the six conditions use moving averages:
    <span class="c">above_200dma</span> compares the close with EMA200, and
    <span class="c">ema21_above_50</span> compares the two shorter averages. The
    <span class="c">ema21</span>, <span class="c">ema50</span> and
    <span class="c">ema200</span> values are in the data, so you can see the
    distances rather than just a yes or no.
  </p>

  <p class="lede">
    Next: <a href="/learn/adx/">ADX</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/rsi/">RSI</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/support-resistance/">support and resistance</a>
  </p>
</Base>
